# Embeddable — what `comptime` is allowed to return.

use std::nat::{ Nat }

## A value that can be embedded in the compiled artifact.
##
## `comptime` runs its body while the compiler is alive and then replaces the
## call site with the result. So the result has to outlive the compiler: it is
## serialized into the program's IR, written to the store, and — for
## `mez build --native` — frozen into the image heap, then rebuilt in a
## different process, possibly on a different machine.
##
## A value can make that crossing when its meaning is fully determined by data
## it carries. It cannot when its meaning depends on:
##
##   - code      — a function value is a code pointer plus captured values
##   - identity  — a mutable cell's point is that it is *that* cell; a copy is
##                 a different cell
##   - liveness  — a `Promise` is a running computation, a `FileHandle` an open
##                 descriptor, a `Foreign` a live guest-language object. All
##                 belong to the compiler process and die with it.
##
## This ability has no operations. Its existence *is* the content: a
## compile-time permission, in the same sense as a marker trait. Nothing is
## called at runtime. There is nothing for an operation to do either — newtypes
## are erased before reification sees them, so by then the value already IS its
## underlying representation.
##
## ## What has an impl
##
## Primitives, records, variants, and the immutable containers derive
## structurally and on demand — you never write those. See
## `canDeriveAbilityStructurally`.
##
## A NEWTYPE must opt in, exactly as it must for `Eq`:
##
##     newtype Meters = Int
##     impl Embeddable for Meters where { }
##
## This is deliberate and it is the whole mechanism. A newtype is absolutely
## opaque — field access is rejected even inside the defining module — so
## deriving `Embeddable` would silently pierce that wall and bake a hidden
## representation into the shipped artifact. `newtype Password = Str` must not
## become embeddable because somebody returned one from a `comptime` by
## accident. The author decides.
##
## It also makes the rule uniform: **no impl means not embeddable.** `Promise`
## is rejected for precisely the same reason `Meters` is before its author opts
## in — nobody wrote the impl. There is no separate "is this opaque" test and no
## list of native types to maintain.
ability Embeddable for 'a where { }

## `Nat` is a newtype over `Int`, so it does not derive — stdlib opts it in
## here. Its underlying representation is a plain integer, which embeds fine.
impl Embeddable for Nat where { }
